WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.320
Tuesday, April 17th is

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.700
tax day this year.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.440
There'll be publicity about income tax day but

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.560
it's misleading because income taxes

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.780
are just a fraction of what we pay.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:12.820
And income taxes

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.940
are arguably way too high

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.440
but they actually make up less than

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.100
half of our personal tax burden.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.920
Kristin Tate just wrote what she calls a

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.980
Field Guide to the American Tax Rip-off.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.170
How Do I Tax Thee explores the many other taxes

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.100
we pay.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.000
It's crazy.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.200
California has a tax on fresh fruit

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.133
that comes from vending machines.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.360
Car Registration

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.820
Entertainment Tax

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.600
Admissions Tax for Movies

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.660
Property Taxes

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.900
Garbage Taxes

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.320
This isn't an exaggeration.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.080
Every time you do anything

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.660
you're really paying taxes.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.420
Rifle Tax

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.620
Plane Ticket Tax

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.460
But these are tiny taxes.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.500
Well some of them actually aren't tiny taxes.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.960
The reason I wrote this book

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.859
is because I was getting charged about $200 a month

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.180
by Comcast for my cable bill.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.989
Thirty-six dollars of it was tax.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:05.840
These kinds of fees are inflating

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.200
every bill we pay.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.580
Why should we believe you?

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.700
You're 12 years old.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.791
I did the research.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.500
All of this information is available online.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.750
The results were shocking.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.755
What do you think you pay in taxes?

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.800
Most people don't know what they pay.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.140
I think it's 22%.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.900
I'm probably paying closer to 20%.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.400
Did you take a plane here?

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.460
I did.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.460
Did you know you paid 20% in taxes on your ticket?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.860
I did not realize it was that much, no.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.500
How much are you paying for your hotel?

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.660
It's about $100 dollars a night.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.580
So about $20 dollars of that

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.580
is going right to taxes.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.580
Wow.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.460
They get us everywhere.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.420
I think I need to get a side job that pays cash.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.439
We need taxes

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:50.320
to pay for government.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.814
Of course we need taxes.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.920
The problem

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.740
is that we're often being taxed

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.500
without even knowing it.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.500
From going to a restaurant with our friends,

00:01:58.504 --> 00:01:59.770
to buying a movie ticket,

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.640
to riding the subway,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.700
we're getting taxed on those expenses.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.000
In Colorado,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:05.940
they tax

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.560
non- essential food items

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.940
like napkins, bibs, and straws.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.960
In New York, they have a bagel cutting tax

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.040
which you get charged for whenever you

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.560
buy a pre-sliced bagel.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.980
The whole bagel you don't pay the tax?

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.300
Yeah you don't pay the tax if you buy the whole bagel so

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.160
I always get my bagels non-sliced to avoid this tax.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.720
This is a left-wing town.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.640
They love high taxes.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.840
It's part of the deal.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.360
If New Yorkers want to pay high taxes,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.940
they can vote for high taxes.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:36.760
My problem

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.671
is the way

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.040
that these taxes are implemented.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.880
Politicians are cowards.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.420
Instead of creating a tax,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.580
they magically create these little fees

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.960
and they don't have to tell their voters

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.260
they raised taxes.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.504
So people don't know.

00:02:49.504 --> 00:02:50.739
I have a list here of

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:53.420
100 different taxes that you pay.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.980
Even they tax me on the beer that I drink.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.520
Uncle Sam just kills me,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.120
you know.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.080
Can you think of anything you do in life

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.680
that is not taxed?

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.980
No, I can't.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.140
When I sleep am I being taxed?

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.340
Everything's taxed,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.240
even when I breathe.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.700
$1.10 increase in the 911 phone tax.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.340
911 fees

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.840
pay for important emergency services.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.260
Actually no.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.880
In most states and cities,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.440
the 911 fees are just going to general funds.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.820
Chicago for instance,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.460
they hiked their 911 fee in 2008.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.650
There was no Olympics in Chicago,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.035
but they kept that fee in place,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.340
and then in 2014 they raised it again.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.560
Once they put them there,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.140
they almost never go away.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.980
Absolutely not.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.960
Government only grows.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.940
Finally, there's the pet licensing tax.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.300
This is one tax

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.980
that Tate and her dog refuse to pay.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.440
In most cities, it doesn't go to dogs,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.160
it just goes to the general fund

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.040
like all of these other fees.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.030
By saying I'm not paying this,

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.400
you're taking a risk.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.400
Some vindictive bureaucrat.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.100
I am taking a risk.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.920
We pay so much of our money to these...

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.760
The dogs put you over the top?

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.960
It's the little things, John.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.510
The little things add up.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.600
What you pay in income tax

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.500
is probably less than half.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.500
your total tax burden.

